      "end_wall": 1792691185.703976,
      "end_mono": 360742.902212375,
      "raw_response": " ANSWER: SUPPORTED\n\nThe abstract provides evidence that pyridostatin (PDS) increases telomere fragility in BRCA2-deficient cells, which are cells with defects in homologous recombination (HR). This increase in telomere fragility suggests that PDS encourages the proliferation of HR-defective cells, as the cells are forced to undergo more divisions due to the destabilization of their telomeres. The abstract also mentions that PDS reduces the proliferation of HR-deficient cells, but this is likely due to the accumulation of DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs) and the activation of checkpoints, leading to cell death or arrest in G2/M phase. Therefore, while PDS may reduce the proliferation of HR-deficient cells, it also encourages their proliferation by inducing telomere instability.\n\nThe source for this information is the scientific article titled \"Targeting BRCA1 and BRCA2 Deficiencies with G-Quadruplex-Interacting Compounds\" (Scecchitano et al., 2018).",
